unchanged.  An interim pair taken on the pre-review patch was superseded by
this one and is not kept; its corpus run survives as
`corpus_run_interim.out.txt`.

## Verdict: PASS — zero observable deviation, on two independent witnesses

- 110 ledger entries in both captures; entry-name sets identical.
- Per LEDGER ENTRY: `prop_ml` (exact ML constructor syntax, all types and
  sorts) byte-identical; `Term.maxidx_of_term` unchanged (all 110 are -1:
  every corpus debt is monomorphic — polymorphic declaration was impossible
  before this patch).  `prop_pretty` byte-identical after normalizing the
  two session-local serial families (below) — the rendering-level witness
  (review adoption R6), a REQUIRED check, not informational.
- Per DEBT FACT: `prop_ml`, tags, shyps, hyps count, and `Thm.maxidx_of`
  ALL identical — zero flips, strictly less deviation than the plan's
  acceptance formula `maxidx' = Int.max (0, maxidx)` allowed.
- The 3 `FACT: NONE` entries (ledger names with no like-named global fact;
  the resource_space creation path) are the same 3 in both captures.

## Root cause of the maxidx formula/observation gap (source trace)

Every noted fact passes through `Goal.norm_result`
(`Generic_Target.notes`, generic_target.ML:363), which ends in
`Drule.zero_var_indexes` (goal.ML:93-97), which ends in
`Thm.adjust_maxidx_thm ~1` (drule.ML:206-220) — the cached maxidx bound is
recomputed exactly.  So section 8.1 C3's chain-level flip (`varifyT_global`
bumps the returned theorem's bound to 0 even when it is the identity) never
reaches the stored fact.  `real-command-transcripts/MaxidxProbe.thy`
exhibits the two endpoints (chain 0, noted -1); the trace above, not the
probe, is the verification.

## The session-local serial families in prop_pretty

`prop_pretty` strings carry PIDE YXML markup with two per-session serial
families: `entity ref=<serial>` (assigned when an entity is printed) and
`def_id=<serial>` (a per-theory-load Document_ID allocated inside the
parallel theory schedule, thy_info.ML:361-363, surfaced by position.ML:232).
`compare_dumps.py` normalizes both and then requires byte equality; the
residue on this pair is zero.  Visible text and structure are identical
either way, and `prop_ml` independently proves the underlying terms equal.

## Scope note

The corpus contains no `discharge_debt_axiom` command, so the dump diff and
the corpus runs are DECLARATION-path evidence only; the discharge path's
evidence is `real-command-transcripts/` (asserted message texts, the
premised/weaker-sort/composite discharges, and the negatives).

## Post-acceptance touch-ups (2026-09-02, re-review findings K2/K3)

After this pair was captured (Debt_Axiom.ML at md5 413e90a2...), two
re-review cleanups changed Debt_Axiom.ML (final md5 d1e1d605...):
K2 replaces the certificate-description position guard with a case on
`Position.here`'s own result — a string builder on the discharge ERROR path,
which the corpus never reaches (no `discharge_debt_axiom` in it); K3 rebuilds
`ledger_form`'s recover closure with the exhaustive `map2` over the
name/sort lists instead of destructuring `strip` — value-identical by
construction, since `strip = map (apply2 TFree) (tfrees ~~ tfrees')` makes
the two traversals enumerate the same pairs in the same order.  Neither can
affect a declaration-path capture, so this pair remains the valid section
8.3-1 artifact for the final code; all real-command transcripts and their
`.out.txt` captures WERE re-run on the final (d1e1d605) code.
